  g10-metal-sgs-z5-ramp5             max         flat_end_mill Z-Carb-HPR Z5 (5 flute rougher square      5    no ramp up to 5 degrees   c     chart materials (steels ... ha
  g10-hobby-012                      recommend   any                                                      -     - 20 degrees             c     any
  g10-hobby-013                      no ramp     any                                                      -    no don't want to ramp or  c     any
  g10-hobby-024                      recommend   flat_end_mill                                            -     - 1.5 degrees or 2.5 deg c     metal (general)
  g10-hobby-025                      max         flat_end_mill                                            -     - 10 to 20 degrees       c     tough materials (metal)

  Summary (derived):
  - wood or router vendor statements at grade a/b: 0
  - every ramp-angle number is grade c: metal / non-ferrous vendors (Harvey, Helical, SGS, IMCO),
    a CAM article (CNCCookbook), or a user post (Carbide Create 20 deg).
  - SGS centre-cutting 2-3 flute and up-cut router: 90 deg (a plunge is allowed); non-centre-cutting
    or 4-7 flute: 1-7 deg; compression router 5 deg; down-cut router '-' (no legend).

  Repo ramp angles:
    dressup (E1 Face/Pocket/Profile/Rest/Zigzag) 3.0 deg   crates/rs_cam_core/src/compute/config.rs:1036
    Adaptive3d (E3, Suggest rewrite to Ramp)     10.0 deg   crates/rs_cam_core/src/compute/operation_configs.rs:832
    CLI ramp (2.5D and Adaptive3d)               3.0 / 3.0 deg
    Pencil internal ramp cap                      12.0 deg
  Position against the metal ranges (context, not a source): 3 deg is the low end and 10 deg the top of
  Harvey/Helical 'soft / non-ferrous 3-10 deg'. 3 deg is below the SGS compression-router maximum (5 deg);
  10 deg is above it. No statement prints a ramp angle for a ball, tapered ball or V-bit in wood.

==============================================================================
T2. Helix size: printed statements in one frame, path radius / D (derived)
==============================================================================
  Frames: bore diameter B, path (tool-centre) diameter P, path radius r. B = P + D; r = P / 2 = (B - D) / 2.
  No core for a flat tool: r <= D/2, so P <= D and B <= 2D. With corner radius rc: r <= D/2 - rc (IMCO 2D - 2rc).

  statement                        frame      bound    r/D         grade  reading
  g10-metal-harvey-te-helix-dia    ambiguous  min      0.05-0.10   c      '>110-120% of the cutter diameter' read as BORE: r = (1.1..1.2 - 1)/2
  g10-metal-helical-gb-helix-dia   ambiguous  min      0.55-0.60   c      same text, read as PATH diameter: r = 1.1..1.2 / 2 (leaves a core)
  g10-metal-imco-bore-2d-2r        bore       max      0.50        c      B = 2D - 2rc; r = D/2 - rc; flat rc = 0
  g10-metal-sandvik-max-hole       bore       max      0.50        c      max hole 2 x D3 (non-centre-cutting inserts): r = D/2
  g10-metal-sandvik-core           bore       -        -           c      a smaller hole-to-cutter ratio leaves a core (text only)
  g10-hobby-018                    path       max      0.50        c      helix diameter must not exceed D (boss stays): r = D/2
  g10-hobby-019                    path       example  0.40        c      figure example 0.8 x Dia (good) vs 1.8 x Dia (boss): r = 0.4 D
  g10-hobby-020                    path       -        -           c      no minimum printed
  Note: the two Harvey/Helical statements print the SAME words; the table shows each of the two readings once.
  Bore reading: r/D 0.05-0.10 (a near-plunge). Path reading: r/D 0.55-0.60 (a core of 0.1-0.2 D for a flat tool).

  Repo helix radius per matrix size, path radius / D (derived):
  kind                  D no-core r/D |  A3d 0.3D dressup 2.0mm CLI3d 0.4D | centre pip height (mm) at A3d / dressup / CLI3d
  EndMill           3.175       0.500 |     0.300         0.630      0.400 | 0 / core 0.82 / 0
  EndMill           6.000       0.500 |     0.300         0.333      0.400 | 0 / 0 / 0
  BullNose          3.175       0.350 |     0.300         0.630      0.400 | 0 / core 1.78 / 0.027
  BullNose          6.000       0.350 |     0.300         0.333      0.400 | 0 / 0 / 0.051
  BallNose          3.175       0.000 |     0.300         0.630      0.400 | 0.317 / core 0.82 / 0.635
  BallNose          6.000       0.000 |     0.300         0.333      0.400 | 0.600 / 0.764 / 1.200
  TaperedBallNose   3.175       0.000 |     0.300         0.630      0.400 | 0.317 / core 0.82 / 0.635
  TaperedBallNose   6.000       0.000 |     0.300         0.333      0.400 | 0.600 / 0.764 / 1.200
  VBit              6.350       0.000 |     0.300         0.315      0.400 | 3.300 / 3.464 / 4.399
  VBit             12.700       0.000 |     0.300         0.157      0.400 | 6.599 / 3.464 / 8.799
  no-core r/D: flat 0.5; bull 0.5 - 0.15 = 0.35; ball, tapered ball, V-bit 0 (any r > 0 leaves a centre pip).
  pip height: flat/bull 0 up to the flat bottom radius; past it a core stands ('core' = its diameter, 2 (r - flat));
  ball / tapered ball R - sqrt(R^2 - r^2) with R = D/2 (core when r > R); V-bit r / tan(30 deg).
  Caution: Adaptive3d multiplies the factor by the ENVELOPE diameter (INVENTORY_G10 1.3); for the tapered ball
  the FM1 diameter is the tip, so the real A3d radius on that tool is larger than the table shows.

