==============================================================================
T5b. The approved design, checked: ramp feed = min(F, a_z x n x Z / tan(theta))
==============================================================================
  With the G6 axial chip a_z = f_z,side / Z (Amana), and the side feed F = f_z,side x n x Z:
    a_z x n x Z = f_z,side x n = F / Z, so the second term = F / (Z tan theta).
  It binds (is below F) only when tan theta > 1/Z. Z = 2: theta > 26.57 deg; Z = 3: theta > 18.43 deg.
  Where the feed F is capped below f_z,side x n x Z (the machine cap 4000), the threshold is higher still.
  Physical check: the vertical rate on a ramp is F_ramp x sin theta (along-path feed). Holding it at the plunge
  F/Z gives F_ramp = F / (Z sin theta); tan and sin differ by cos theta (0.14 % at 3 deg, 1.5 % at 10 deg).

  theta (deg) arm                         1/(Z tan) Z=2    Z=3  SGS (metal)
         1.00 SGS lower                           28.64  19.10         1.00
         2.00 SGS 'slotting feed'                 14.32   9.55         1.00
         3.00 dressup ramp                         9.54   6.36            -
         4.55 dressup helix 2.0 mm / 1.0           6.28   4.19            -
         6.00 SGS '25 %'                           4.76   3.17         0.25
        10.00 A3d ramp                             2.84   1.89            -
        18.48 A3d helix, D 3.175                   1.50   1.00            -
        12.00 pencil cap                           2.35   1.57            -
        20.00 Carbide Create (user post)           1.37   0.92            -
  Values > 1 mean the approved form ships the full cutting feed F.
  SGS vertical rate (derived, frac x sin theta, fraction of slot feed): 1 deg 0.017, 2 deg 0.035, 6 deg 0.026;
  the SGS plunge is 0.25. So SGS holds its ramp far below its own plunge rate: its rule is not an axial-chip rule.

